*.syso binary
*.ico binary

# Go source files always use LF, even on Windows checkouts with
# core.autocrlf=true. Without this, gofmt's CRLF→LF normalisation
# fights the Git filter on every save and produces noisy diffs.
*.go text eol=lf

# Web assets, JS/TS, JSON, and config we control are LF too — same reasoning.
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# The citation checker's allowlist (internal/docs) is read by a test that trims
# each line, so CRLF would still parse; this pin is hygiene against git's
# "will become CRLF" warning on a fresh Windows checkout. Path-specific on
# purpose: a blanket *.txt would renormalise unrelated files.
internal/docs/citation_allowlist.txt text eol=lf

# The Go toolchain writes go.mod/go.sum with LF, but core.autocrlf=true gives
# them CRLF in the working tree — so `go mod tidy -diff` reported a 329-line
# whole-file diff with zero content change, which ruled it out as a CI gate.
# Pinning both to LF makes that gate usable (ci.yml runs it).
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf

# tools/fetch-node rewrites this tracked manifest with LF; without the pin a
# core.autocrlf=true checkout shows it modified with identical content.
internal/bgutils/embed/version.txt text eol=lf

# Docker files are consumed on Linux — keep LF on Windows checkouts too.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Windows-only batch and PowerShell scripts: keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
